#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133531 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/id #uuid "cb31a55d-6107-509a-80e2-9bdffcaff6e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5590" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/assets/98b9eaa366bb0c1dcf899eb231b34e9704335a294d4b69c6af10197eb14929ce.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf{b}$ shown above. Find the general least-squares solution of the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T \\! A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 54\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -4 \\\\ -18 & 54 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\nx_1=-\\dfrac{2}{3}+3x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/assets/b36e5a28c59c1ab0be811fb94fec4dc018b6687229f4acb3ef11a745fdeb6631.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133532 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/id #uuid "c5097abf-c541-5c52-85d3-1e56a4e53cb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5591" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. Find the general least-squares solution of the system $A \\mathbf x = \\mathbf b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/assets/8803a165894746f62c8d27530eebd791d03cfc16ee8da0dd3e176c30a400ee4a.png)\n\nThe least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T \\! A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & -36 \\\\ -36 & 144\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -36 & 3 \\\\ -36 & 144 & -12\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & -36 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac{1}{3}+ 4x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/assets/1f35d807d723ae25214ce1cdd9fdb42c5111f6a0495a5f453e8a04b415863466.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133557 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System"}] :question/id #uuid "8a39f109-9436-55f8-bbb8-87253668378d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5963" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/assets/86e05af9d6062530450e56f5e292106413301a869e765f413dcbe6865a6ce8a5.png)\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a-b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071217/assets/c2daef6c8fee0dd47562395ec76a2912eaea98e3a873d6319553ed281745d210.png)\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & -2\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}80 & -60 & -20 \\\\ -60 & 45 & 15 \\\\ -20 & 15 & 5\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}32 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na-b+c=(-20)-(-24)+(-20) = -16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab465d1c-344a-52fe-8324-f06b554b1dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369852} {:answer/id #uuid "c553329f-bced-55e1-908c-cf4cdd179dec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369853} {:answer/id #uuid "d956d1d9-f58d-534f-8a3b-81190c74c326" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369854} {:answer/id #uuid "82d46159-f769-550b-a175-7f32599fd28e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369855} {:answer/id #uuid "41ac6592-7e5c-54ff-b8ad-6bbac24d815f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41ac6592-7e5c-54ff-b8ad-6bbac24d815f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369856} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3c59ad2-e391-55a6-8558-ac957147cb06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f2dd3f5-5fe5-5a62-8afe-17692ba63240" :question/math-academy-id "q-136811" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ above. If the normal equation corresponding to the system $Ax=b$ is given below, what is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 20 \\\\ 20 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10 & 20 \\\\ 20 & 40\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=10+40+(-2)=48.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85503509-bf55-5fb0-8f88-89056e873bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369859} {:answer/id #uuid "97aee592-b3f2-5ce6-8127-56ca42e08d01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369860} {:answer/id #uuid "cb289323-3b53-5217-9813-72c07984b2c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369861} {:answer/id #uuid "65f8c321-f324-5f99-ae6e-544d64d5608b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369862} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc3176a-c9ca-5867-91c7-29b0c8c9e31f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bc3176a-c9ca-5867-91c7-29b0c8c9e31f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369863} :answer-field/id #uuid "e99c7c9f-200e-5f63-a7d0-c9c8edfc8422" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09f1bdf0-428b-503d-8931-22307697fe1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-136822" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}=1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}40 & -20 & -60 \\\\ -20 & 10 & a \\\\ c & 30 & 90\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ b \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}40 & 20 & -60 \\\\ -20 & 10 & 30 \\\\ -60 & 30 & 90\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=30+5+(-60)=-25.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1f6d009-fac9-58b6-aa21-757bd870ac4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369866} {:answer/id #uuid "5512c8c1-32e0-532c-a4e3-03776eb3e233" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369867} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b4b50c-ba9e-51da-95a9-e1b022fa7a59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369868} {:answer/id #uuid "12a8ecaf-4145-5481-ac58-5eeb3157cc2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369869} {:answer/id #uuid "70d5a9ce-a337-55e9-af7e-f7351726eb18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5b4b50c-ba9e-51da-95a9-e1b022fa7a59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369868} :answer-field/id #uuid "4af9faf3-c6d8-5537-8122-6a64458e0ecb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d938ee92-d65b-59e1-9582-a9b03b4f837e" :question/math-academy-id "q-136875" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=-3\\end{aligned}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}20 & a & 10 \\\\ c & 80 & -20 \\\\ 10 & -20 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ b \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a-b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}20 & -40 & 10 \\\\ -40 & 80 & -20 \\\\ 10 & -20 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ -44 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\na-b+c=(-40)-(-44)+(-40)=-36.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df9f2763-d39d-5223-8e39-4d3092c917cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369873} {:answer/id #uuid "4e875a8b-4b2e-5b29-b08b-ed445264c8f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369874} {:answer/id #uuid "37e602a9-c414-51db-8fca-d21ba1a2a571" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369875} {:answer/id #uuid "4a528e8f-cecc-53f4-b13a-c4d7eec37ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369876} {:answer/id #uuid "f2591de9-2046-545c-8f70-b2c8dee86934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2591de9-2046-545c-8f70-b2c8dee86934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369877} :answer-field/id #uuid "c70e5158-2ece-57ae-acb2-c7306756f29a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2152687-9e2e-5018-abbd-8ed5e8bc9188" :question/math-academy-id "q-137034" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,+\\,{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ c \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+2b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.\n\nFrom the second row, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{5}-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the first row, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+2b+c=1+2\\cdot 0+(-1)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65fe1574-7033-5bc7-a434-ec17d86082cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369880} {:answer/id #uuid "bdecd056-0de1-55de-b344-ad53dfbb81d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369881} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf2cd19-f992-55fd-bcfb-b7e3d5e090c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369882} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f5890d-287e-559b-a09f-bdb23c7df160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369883} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d53099-f8df-53c9-b5b9-81f2c39376a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccf2cd19-f992-55fd-bcfb-b7e3d5e090c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369882} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7c75a6e-e41a-5989-94e6-598bbbf5adae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac571b42-2983-530f-a451-ee231a94a9ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-137061" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}30 & -60 \\\\ -60 & 120\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n2a+\\frac{b}{c}=2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{2}{1}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bab8db1-d2da-528f-9e7f-2a61537f6385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369887} {:answer/id #uuid "1491407e-efa4-59cf-ad95-3f9891740e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369888} {:answer/id #uuid "1ffd1f45-4a38-585e-90ce-993f8f9363da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369889} {:answer/id #uuid "a83b648a-5830-5fa6-b87c-fa641a89ecd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369890} {:answer/id #uuid "3c93b656-5eb6-52f0-8f33-c98f9187438f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bab8db1-d2da-528f-9e7f-2a61537f6385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369887} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9eacad7-7eea-5ce0-9696-fbb4c7cbeb50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e6af293-22e2-57dc-ac36-0c68460f20c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-137095" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $4a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}14 & -14 \\\\ -14 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -14 & 7 \\\\ -14 & 14 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}14 & -14 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n4a+\\frac{b}{c}=4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{1}{1}=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24f5c83d-7742-549e-be99-81aef55cc29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369894} {:answer/id #uuid "b55251a9-c931-5eb6-baf8-3966a28e2642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369895} {:answer/id #uuid "a169034c-2bd9-55d8-ac40-25818b93c57f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369896} {:answer/id #uuid "44630438-796f-528e-9dc5-cd29f6652f8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369897} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d94d77-a9af-562b-ba06-cc2e45c6044c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0d94d77-a9af-562b-ba06-cc2e45c6044c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369898} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7f8a06e-80d1-5b8b-a269-2db2dc661081" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85757ebe-6f7f-5aa8-9cf4-901c45e3eda8" :question/math-academy-id "q-137096" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a-\\frac{3b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 \\\\ 18 & 36\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 & -9 \\\\ 18 & 36 & -18\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-1-2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na-\\frac{3b}{c}=-1-\\frac{3(-2)}{1}=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2346a291-928a-5965-a144-7ecc1ea32e06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369901} {:answer/id #uuid "d44359c3-00f3-50d6-a486-c6b901c5a662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369902} {:answer/id #uuid "b0198586-398c-5fe4-abeb-c098826a5744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369903} {:answer/id #uuid "ed082553-0997-5289-9588-a01c997c26a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369904} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a2e42c-ac8c-5a09-a4b5-31575b584f90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6a2e42c-ac8c-5a09-a4b5-31575b584f90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369905} :answer-field/id #uuid "06fb5ca0-97cf-599d-9d3f-54d5bb3dd03e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e13f62f-4b2e-5e53-998a-b4040c277b68" :question/math-academy-id "q-137119" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $7a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}14 & -28 \\\\ -28 & 56\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -28 & -4 \\\\ -28 & 56 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}14 & -28 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{7}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{7}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n7a+\\frac{b}{c}=7\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{7})+\\frac{2}{1}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "102334fb-281d-51f1-b08d-10b9377b4e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369908} {:answer/id #uuid "9c6537be-6fbc-5557-bcce-2f147f2f8f83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369909} {:answer/id #uuid "26636dac-94dc-54b3-937a-f5d8b64501fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369910} {:answer/id #uuid "e330fe56-a13c-5659-9bdf-799bd795e6c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369911} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c1ca17-575b-58fd-9f9c-452a472af309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26636dac-94dc-54b3-937a-f5d8b64501fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369910} :answer-field/id #uuid "e854affc-e92d-556a-a578-fede17e66498" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79003c00-4845-59ab-90de-beecf1933548" :question/math-academy-id "q-40921" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $3a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 & 12 \\\\ -18 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n3a+\\frac{b}{c}=3\\cdot \\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})}{1}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86235d04-2f54-56c9-addd-095242f52c59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131991} {:answer/id #uuid "2fa3a517-2398-596d-8530-6e9ce8cf4426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131992} {:answer/id #uuid "4f271ffd-ca62-5430-9bda-65d6826ab1e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131993} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9cf598-e066-51ba-8137-1d8ac71b82c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131994} {:answer/id #uuid "7438a4ee-7a1f-547b-9559-806b7e930ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e9cf598-e066-51ba-8137-1d8ac71b82c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131994} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed53e70b-1aef-5e4b-b7f8-b4a7e0a45fc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "871a1be1-8f4a-5616-a788-c400e98cd9b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-40943" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+2b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 12 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 12 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -8 & 12 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}-4(\\frac{3}{2}+{x}_{3})+2{x}_{3}=-2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2+{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+2b+c=1+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+0=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369913 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bbe17dd-7e72-5f62-8dcf-2d00de8661f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369915} {:answer/id #uuid "d9205714-e382-5ab1-8e7f-4434e2b7169a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369916} {:answer/id #uuid "6a235c48-ebd4-5d96-ac78-445ebe1ca8b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369917} {:answer/id #uuid "a2c9826a-eadb-561f-80d0-9d2505adba9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369918} {:answer/id #uuid "7db03a37-c5cb-50be-b600-490746188877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7db03a37-c5cb-50be-b600-490746188877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369919} :answer-field/id #uuid "43770019-6138-5aa7-b875-447631063bc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c15c93a-202f-5422-b992-46722d220d17" :question/math-academy-id "q-40950" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,+\\,{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $2a-3b-6c?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(1/6){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=(1/2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable.  \n\nFrom the third row, we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{3}=1,\n$$\n\nand from the first row we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n2a-3b-6c=2(\\frac{1}{2})-3(1)-6(0)=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17f4d85f-e5be-5639-8dff-430b0bca6149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369922} {:answer/id #uuid "e1987bec-aa0b-5a03-bf0a-603217b752f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369923} {:answer/id #uuid "1b632fe9-0e26-5cde-a4c2-ff05071075ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369924} {:answer/id #uuid "4710f6eb-ec31-5b96-84c2-8c05baa3b934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369925} {:answer/id #uuid "093156e5-0a7f-5f70-83e8-6db2dd14ae10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1987bec-aa0b-5a03-bf0a-603217b752f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369923} :answer-field/id #uuid "53f2c11b-a452-53f6-ac7f-a938d466be03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e60c5a85-11ba-5c20-a998-994d66fa6373" :question/math-academy-id "q-41904" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $15a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}40 & -20 \\\\ -20 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -10 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}10 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n15a+\\frac{b}{c}=15\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1}=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d632ba55-0b60-52e6-be20-c84340c0fa37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132225} {:answer/id #uuid "fd996c99-8224-5079-9bbe-9b01e2197fb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132226} {:answer/id #uuid "0ddeff2a-b870-5cf7-9269-a51e457fffb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132227} {:answer/id #uuid "16c9029b-cf7c-5215-8cad-b358ce284029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132228} {:answer/id #uuid "481b5d9a-50f6-55f7-aa3f-886cf8db5248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16c9029b-cf7c-5215-8cad-b358ce284029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132228} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bb929cb-ab52-57e4-ba68-ac64a46a5dc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80e97aa1-dac8-53af-afe4-09e324e932eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-41909" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=5 \\\\ 14{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}245 & 70 & a \\\\ 70 & 20 & 10 \\\\ c & 10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}35 \\\\ b \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 14 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}7 & 14 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 14 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 14 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}245 & 70 & 35 \\\\ 70 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 35 & 10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}35 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=35+10+35=80.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99d52566-eada-54af-8610-f6b1934d1957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369928} {:answer/id #uuid "0b3303f2-8c1a-5a5d-9904-5e4aeeab52c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369929} {:answer/id #uuid "d7ae1f96-0be1-537a-ba9a-ab9ba0a21268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369930} {:answer/id #uuid "61d4bd6f-7ced-585f-9835-ed2c4a1536c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369931} {:answer/id #uuid "cae12a7f-986c-5eff-8a42-7a4ed7ba4c1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cae12a7f-986c-5eff-8a42-7a4ed7ba4c1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369932} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7f1636c-f244-53c9-875a-115c6cf53f35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0680aa30-4775-5c1b-82c5-b722226f12db" :question/math-academy-id "q-41912" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ above. If the normal equation corresponding to the system $Ax=b$ is given below, what is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 21 \\\\ 21 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}21 & 21 \\\\ 21 & 21\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=21+21+6=48.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134299 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e47dda1-f0a7-502b-a445-0181c3b49f69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132237} {:answer/id #uuid "b3e1e31d-d467-51f5-96ef-503eca2d166a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132238} {:answer/id #uuid "c13ef682-c01d-5710-9973-f17e3c2c9707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132239} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0c8de0-76a3-57b3-972b-35aadc11c0a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132240} {:answer/id #uuid "3d07b396-77b6-53a8-a384-0e1bd1724268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3e1e31d-d467-51f5-96ef-503eca2d166a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132238} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa322946-7e0d-5934-95b4-40e6e80fc536" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00e4806c-b4ce-579d-8d0e-03b84320da38" :question/math-academy-id "q-41919" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $30a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}30 & -30 \\\\ -30 & 30\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}30 & -30 & 1 \\\\ -30 & 30 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}30 & -30 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{30}+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{30}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{30} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n30a+\\frac{b}{c}=30\\cdot \\frac{1}{30}+\\frac{1}{1}=2.\n$$"}]}
